inue\", \"next_reflection\": f\"adjust-{len(attempts)}\"}. This is deterministic and uses only the case payload.\n\nWe must write Python source as a string. The function must be named `solve`. We'll define it.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    attempts = payload['attempts']\n    max_attempts = payload['max_attempts']\n    # Check for success in any attempt; typical case: last attempt success\n    # We'll stop if any success occurs\n    for attempt in attempts:\n        if attempt['success']:\n            return {\"action\": \"stop\", \"reason\": \"success\"}\n    # No success\n    if len(attempts) >= max_attempts:\n        return {\"action\": \"stop\", \"reason\": \"max_attempts\"}\n    else:\n        next_reflection = f\"adjust-{len(attempts)}\"\n        return {\"action\": \"continue\", \"next_reflection\": next_reflection}\n```\n\nThis uses no external libraries, does not mutate case, returns a dict. It's JSON-serializable.\n\nBut we need to ensure we handle edge cases: if attempts list is empty? Then no success, len(attempts) < max_attempts, next_reflection = \"adjust-0\". That's fine.\n\nNow we need to output a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source as a JSON string. So we must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. We'll do that properly.\n\nMake sure the entire response is a valid JSON object. No extra text.\n\nSo final answer: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    attempts = payload['attempts']\\n    max_attempts = payload['max_attempts']\\n    for attempt in attempts:\\n        if attempt['success']:\\n            return {\\\"action\\\": \\\"stop\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"success\\\"}\\n    if len(attempts) >= max_attempts:\\n        return {\\\"action\\\": \\\"stop\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"max_attempts\\\"}\\n    else:\\n        next_reflection = f\\\"adjust-{len(attempts)}\\\"\\n        return {\\\"action\\\": \\\"continue\\\", \\\"